{
  "passage": "A plant must move water from its roots to leaves that may be many metres above the ground, and it does this without a pump. The water travels through a tissue called xylem. Xylem is made up of long tubes formed from cells joined end to end. Surprisingly, the main cells of the xylem are dead when they do their job. As they mature, they lose their living contents, and the cross walls between them break down, leaving hollow, continuous pipes. Their thick walls, strengthened by a substance called lignin, keep the pipes from collapsing and also help the stem to stand upright. Water enters the roots from the soil and is pulled up through these tubes. The main force that pulls it is transpiration, the loss of water vapour through tiny pores called stomata on the leaves. As water evaporates from the leaves, it creates a pull that draws more water up the xylem, like drinking through a straw. A second tissue, the phloem, carries food made in the leaves to other parts of the plant, such as the roots and fruits. Unlike xylem, the main cells of phloem are living. Together, xylem and phloem form the vascular bundles that can be seen as veins in a leaf. A large tree may lose hundreds of litres of water on a hot day through transpiration.",
